## PO 001 Build Cyber Operation Software Tools

	- Performance: Build Cyber Operations software tools.

	- Conditions:

	- given:

		- problem/goal/optimization opportunity;

		- target network connectivity;

		- open-source internet connection;

		- administrator credentials;

		- Information Management (IM) / Information Technologies (IT) and ancillary equipment;

		- network sensors;

		- software and tools;

		- network diagrams;

		- references;

		- suitable work area; and

		- supervision;

	- denied: nil; and

	- environment: day or night, under any weather conditions, within a technical environment.

	- Standard: The Senior Cyber Def Analyst shall build cyber operations software tools IAW refs (D38, D39, D40, D41, D44 and D45) by:

	- interpreting operating system (OS) fundamentals, to include:

		- Linux fundamentals (file system and permissions, libraries);

		- windows fundamentals (kernel modules, file system, registry, active directory Group Policy Objects (GPOs);

		- principles of OS (processes & threads, memory mgt);

		- Linux command line utilities;

		- Bourne Again Shell (BASH) shell scripts;

		- windows GPOs; and 

		- Linux file Permissions;

	- applying mathematics fundamentals, to include:

		- number systems and logic (short review);

		- number systems - representation & conversion;

		- binary arithmetic;

		- Boolean algebra;

		- linear algebra (short review);

		- functions & equations;

		- matrices;

		- equation solving with matrices;

		- applied probability & statistics (short review);

		- probability theory;

		- probability distributions;

		- basic statistical measures;

		- curve fitting;

		- distribution fitting; and

		- hypothesis testing;

	- (FT0340) building custom testing environment, to include:

		- domain name servers (DNS);

		- web servers and proxies;

		- mail & file servers;

		- active directory domain controller; and 

		- (ET0010) maintaining a collection database;

	- applying mathematics for cryptography and statistical analysis, to include:

		- applying machine learning math, to include:

		- linear algebra

		- statistical norms, means, std deviations, hypothesis testing; and

		- probability concepts; 

		- applying machine learning algorithms, to include:

		- regression;

		- decision trees;

		- clustering; and 

		- basics of deep learning;

		- implementing a Transport Layer Security (TLS) / Secure Socket Layer (SSL) key exchange and encryption system from primitives, to include:

		- block and stream ciphers;

		- block chaining;

		- asymmetric algorithms; and 

		- hashing;

	- (FT0280) developing software tools to include:

		- applying C programming techniques to include:

		- implementing a statistical algorithm; and 

		- implementing data encoding techniques.

		- applying Object Oriented Programming (OOP) principles, to include:

		- object oriented programming;

		- classes and objects;

		- methods and interfaces;

		- relationships; 

		- inheritance; and

		- polymorphism;

		- designing a software solution, to include:

		- object oriented analysis; and 

		- object oriented design;

		- writing custom tools, to include:

		- applying a machine learning algorithm;

		- developing a custom network testing toolset;

		- implementing a web server; and

		- implementing secure Diffie-Hellman (DH) key exchange and Advanced Encryption Standard - Counter Mode (AES-CTR) encryption from primitives;

	- applying software development methodologies to include:

		- (FT0300) maintaining custom network toolsets;

		- (FT0290) testing custom network tool; and

		- estimating development time and effort.

	- Remarks: Recommendations to Training Plan and development of the content:

	- the following knowledge should be included as teaching points:

		- the C program constructs (such as control statements, loops, functions, pointers, and data structures), data types, and memory management should be covered for C programming techniques; and

		- the processes and products, complexity measures, source code control and review, and configuration management for software development methodologies.

## PO 002 Analyze Windows Enterprise Networks

		- Performance: Analyze Windows enterprise networks.

		- Conditions:

		- given:

		- Internet Protocol (IP) system traffic, host logs and data;

		- open-source internet connection;

		- IM/IT and ancillary equipment;

		- endpoint sensors;

		- software and tools;

		- network diagrams;

		- references;

		- work area; and 

		- supervision;

		- denied: alerts; and

		- environment: day or night, under any weather conditions, within a technical environment.

		- Standard: The Sr Cyber Def Analyst shall analyze Windows enterprise networks IAW refs (D49 and D50) by:

		- (GT0080) analyzing network traffic, to include:

		- identifying attacks, to include:

		- defense evasion, to include:

		- encryption;

		- masquerading; and

		- obfuscation;

		- Command and Control, to include:

		- beaconing;

		- lateral movement;

		- HyperText Transfer Protocol (HTTP) communication ;

		- DNS communication; and

		- other protocols;

		- identifying communication protocols, to include: 

		- DNS;

		- HTTP(s);

		- Server Message Block (SMB);

		- Secure Shell (SSH);

		- Kerberos; and

		- modern communication protocols including but not limited to:

		- TLS 1.2/1.3; and 

	- Quick User Datagram Protocol (UDP) Internet Connection (QUIC), DNS over TLS (DoT), DNS over HTTPS (DOH); and

		- collecting metadata from network traffic;

		- analyzing host logs, to include:

		- identifying attacks, to include:

		- persistence techniques, to include:

		- legitimate AutoStart mechanisms;

		- uncommon AutoStart mechanisms;

		- account manipulation techniques; and

		- process hijacking techniques;

		- defense evasion, to include:

		- autoruns evasion;

		- event log evasion;

		- Endpoint Detection and Response (EDR) evasion;

		- Antivirus (AV) evasion; and

		- evidence removal;

		- selecting relevant Windows host and Active Directory (AD) logs, to include:

		- AD overview and logging, to include:

		- security and audit logs;

		- logon events;

		- other logs relevant to security logs; and

		- AD protocols;

		- Windows host logs, to include:

		- built-in events; and

		- Sysmon;

		- collecting metadata from host logs, to include:

		- Windows event forwarding and alternatives;

		- GPOs; and

		- agents;

		- identifying exploits in host logs, to include:

		- binary exploits; and

		- Windows exploits;

		- identifying persistence in host logs, to include:

		- establishing persistence; and

		- executing from persistence; 

		- identifying indicators of cyber activity, to include:

		- (GT0070) characterizing network traffic by establishing normal patterns, to include:

		- pattern of life; and

		- admin practices;

		- characterizing host logs by establishing normal patterns, to include:

		- pattern of life; and

		- admin practices;

		- (GT0110) analyzing anomalies in network traffic using metadata;

		- analyzing anomalies in host logs using metadata, to include:

		- AD logs;

		- Windows logs; and 

		- Sysmon;

		- visualizing data;

		- developing anomaly detection rules with machine learning and statistics;

		- identifying Tactics, Techniques, and Procedures (TTPs) of malicious activity;

		- reporting on TTPs of malicious activity;

		- (FT0160) developing network sensor rules, to include:

		- writing rules based on, to include:

		- heuristics; and

		- network signatures;

		- (FT0180) testing network sensor rules functionality, to include:

		- volume of false positives;

		- volume of false negatives; and

		- modifying rules to fix identified issues.

		- Remarks: Normal is defined as the contextually expected behaviour of an endpoint or network segment. E.g., a workstation generates at most 10 dns requests per minute therefore, 1000 dns requests in a given minute are deemed not normal (Ref D37).

## PO 003 Conduct Cyber Threat Emulation

		- Performance: Conduct cyber threat emulation.

		- Conditions:

		- given:

		- target network connectivity;

		- open-source internet connection;

		- IM/IT and ancillary equipment;

		- software and tools;

		- network diagrams;

		- references;

		- suitable work area; and 

		- supervision;

		- denied: nil; and

		- environment: day or night, under any weather conditions, within a technical environment.

		- Standard: The Sr Cyber Def Analyst shall conduct cyber threat emulation IAW refs (D46 and D47) by:

		- (ET0080) scanning for network vulnerabilities by:

		- (GT0100) assessing technical risk and vulnerability of cyber systems, to include:

		- using automated scanning tools; and

		- using custom scanning tools;

		- (BT0280) assessing extent of identified vulnerabilities;

		- (BT0260) gaining access to a target device/network environment by:

		- exploiting Linux systems, to include:

		- Set Owner User ID (SUID);

		- interruptible path; and

		- time of check/time of use vulnerabilities;

		- exploiting web systems, to include:

		- Structured Query Language (SQL) injection;

		- SQL filter evasion; and

		- Cross-Site Request Forgery / Cross-Site Scripting (CSRF/XSS);

		- exploiting windows systems, to include:

		- kerberoasting;

		- over privileged accounts; and

		- privilege escalation;

		- (BT0270) generating authorized adverse effects on target network/device, to include:

		- binary exploitation, to include:

		- buffering overflows;

		- formatting string; and

		- Return Oriented Programming (ROP) chains;

		- pivoting within a target network;

		- using exploitation frameworks (e.g., Metasploit);

		- Denial of Service (DOS);

		- encrypt for effect;

		- exfiltrate data;

		- deface for effect; and

		- resource theft.

		- Remarks: This PO is focused solely on exploitation and not on stealth.

## PO 004 Analyze Malware

		- Performance: Analyze malware.

		- Conditions:

		- given:

		- malware samples;

		- analysis environment;

		- open-source internet connection;

		- IM/IT and ancillary equipment;

		- software and tools;

		- references;

		- suitable work area; and 

		- supervision;

		- denied: nil; and

		- environment: day or night, under any weather conditions, within a technical environment.

		- Standard: The Sr Cyber Def Analyst shall analyze malware IAW refs (D25 and D42) by:

		- (BT0400) triaging malware by:

		- (GT0370) conducting basic static analysis, to include:

		- strings;

		- imports;

		- binary characteristics such as size and entropy; and 

		- attributing software behaviours to a particular OS;

		- (GT0390) conducting basic dynamic analysis by:

		- (GT0400) creating synthetic environment by:

		- setting up a safe environment;

		- faking a real environment;

		- setting up logging; and

		- running malware;

		- (GT0380) determining the key characteristics of malicious software, to include:

		- (GT0420) conducting advanced static analysis to include:

		- x86 and x64 assembly;

		- disassemblers;

		- C constructs in assembly; and

		- assembly code;

		- (GT0440) conducting advanced dynamic analysis to include:

		- malware execution environments;

		- techniques to hide execution;

		- debugging malware;

		- patching malware;

		- modifying the environment; and

		- combining advanced static and dynamic analysis, to include:

		- identifying attempts to hide execution;

		- identifying checks for debuggers; and

		- modifying code and environment to run malware;

		- identifying advanced anti-analysis techniques to include:

		- obfuscation techniques, to include:

		- identifying data encoding;

		- identifying packers;

		- anti-disassembly;

		- anti-debugging; and

		- finding tail jumps and other signs of unpacking;

		- defeating advanced anti-analysis techniques to include:

		- defeating anti-analysis, to include: 

		- manually unpacking malware;

		- decoding custom encoders;

		- defeating anti-disassembly;

		- defeating anti-debugging; and

		- analyzing difficult malware.

		- (GT0520) compiling technical intelligence from analyzed malicious software by:

		- conducting malware triage;

		- identifying key characteristics;

		- identifying anti-analysis techniques;

		- defeating anti-analysis techniques; and

		- producing a technical report for distribution.

		- Remarks: The expectation of a Sr Cyber Def Analyst is that they can identify obvious malware, identify obvious benign software, and sending anything else to a specialist for further analysis. The Sr Cyber Def Analyst will be exposed to anti-forensics and anti-analysis techniques, as well as techniques to defeat these, but is not expected to reliably apply these techniques in real world tasks.

## PO 005 Analyze Digital Artifacts

		- Performance: Analyze digital artifacts.

		- Conditions:

		- given:

		- digital media;

		- target network connectivity;

		- open-source internet connection;

		- administrator credentials;

		- IM/IT and ancillary equipment;

		- software and tools;

		- network diagrams;

		- references;

		- suitable work area; and

		- supervision;

		- denied: nil; and

		- environment: day or night, under any weather conditions, within a technical environment.

		- Standard: The Sr Cyber Def Analyst shall analyze digital artifacts for indications of compromise IAW refs (D43, D51 and D52) by:

		- (BT0510) performing static media analysis by:

		- (BT0490) performing file system forensics analysis, to include:

		- volumes and partition;

		- (BT0470) documenting original condition of digital forensic artifacts;

		- identifying artifacts for digital forensics analysis (DFA);

		- identifying operating systems based on network traffic; and

		- (BT0430) recovering artifacts for DFA;

		- conducting OS forensics, to include:

		- on both Windows and Linux, to include:

		- persistence mechanisms;

		- Auto runs; and 

		- Non autoruns;

		- evidence of execution;

		- system logs (windows events, Linux audit, etc.);

		- external device usage;

		- account usage;

		- evidence of credential misuse;

		- privilege escalation;

		- configuration changes;

		- browser usage; and

		- service logs;

		- (GT0220) analyzing digital media for potential cyber incident by:

		- producing an activity timeline;

		- (BT0450) extracting artifacts for DFA;

		- (BT0420) reconstructing a malicious activity;

		- (BT0520) performing digital forensic timeline analysis; and

		- (GT0510) extracting malicious artifacts from other digital media;

		- conducting live forensics by:

		- conducting live artifact acquisition to include:

		- using a remote forensics toolkit such as f response; and

		- using a Digital Forensics and Incident Response (DFIR) toolkit such as GRR Rapid Response, osquery, or velociraptor;

		- conducting live memory acquisition;

		- (GT0500) conducting memory forensics, to include:

		- malicious processes; 

		- kernel forensics and rootkits; and

		- (BT0440) duplicating digital media for forensic;

		- (GT0470) defeating adversary attempts to hide malicious content and behaviour by:

		- identifying log removal;

		- identifying evidence removal;

		- identifying use of poorly instrumented administrative tools; and

		- defeating evidence removal with alternate investigative methods;

		- (CT0210) recommending digital media recovery measures; and

		- (FT0330) maintaining forensics malware lab hardware and software.

		- Remarks: The performance standard for this PO is expected to be practical, a candidate must demonstrate their ability to collect and analyze relevant artifacts and not just know of their existence.

## PO 006 Conduct Advanced Analysis and Exploitation

		- Performance: Conduct advanced analysis and exploitation.

		- Conditions:

		- given:

		- target network connectivity;

		- internet connection;

		- IM/IT and ancillary equipment;

		- network sensors;

		- software and tools;

		- network diagrams;

		- references;

		- suitable work area; and

		- supervision;

		- denied: nil; and

		- environment: day or night, under any weather conditions, within a technical environment.

		- Standard: The Sr Cyber Def Analyst shall conduct advanced analysis and exploitation IAW refs (D43, D46, D47, D48, D51 and D52) by:

		- explaining attacks on mis-implemented cryptography, to include:

		- attacking a vulnerable block cipher mode such as AES - Electronic Code Book (ECB) / Cipher block chaining (CBC) to include explaining importance of CTR / Galois/Counter Mode (GCM);

		- attacking pseudo random number generator such as MTRNG to include explaining importance of cryptographic random number generation; and

		- attacking textbook Rivest-Shamir-Adleman (RSA) algorithm with small e (Bleichenbacher) to include explaining importance of e and padding;

		- (FT0310) developing custom network exploitation tools by:

		- identifying vulnerability;

		- developing exploit for vulnerability;

		- (FT0320) testing custom exploit tool functionality; and

		- modifying existing tool and exploit source code;

		- identifying tool indicators by: 

		- hiding network activity by:

		- identifying normal network activity;

		- configuring C2 profile by hand and statistically;

		- using redirectors;

		- tunneling C2 through legitimate sites; and

		- modifying intra request timing, to include:

		- frequency; and

		- jitter;

		- controlling tool indicators by:

		- hiding network activity by:

		- identifying normal network activity;

		- configuring C2 profile by hand and statistically;

		- using redirectors;

		- tunneling C2 through legitimate sites; and

		- modifying intra request timing, to include:

		- frequency; and

		- jitter;

		- hiding host activity by:

		- moving laterally to include:

		- identifying abnormal activity; and

		- conducting lateral movement while mimicking normal login activity;

		- hiding process execution by:

		- processing injection;

		- masquerading;

		- processing hiding; and

		- attacking only memory;

		- living off the land by:

		- identifying abnormal administrator activity; and

		- using existing OS functionality to mimic normal administrator activity;

		- establishing stealthy persistence (e.g., not detected by auto runs), to include:

		- service hijacking;

		- Dynamic Link Library (DLL) sideloading; and

		- hidden privileged accounts;

		- (GT0040) identifying means of compromise by:

		- (GT0490) defeating anti-forensics techniques, to include:

		- detecting process injection, hiding, and masquerading;

		- statistically detecting living off the land techniques; and

		- identifying stealthy persistence techniques;

		- generating extra features (e.g., intra request times, header sizes, etc.);

		- modelling communication streams using techniques from PO1 statistical analysis;

		- encrypting Communication Analysis; and

		- analyzing login activity to find lateral movement;

		- (GT0160) evaluating adversary TTPs by:

		- building an attack plan using known adversary TTPs;

		- conducting pentest on friendly force following attack plan;

		- evaluating success of pentest; and

		- evaluating likely success of plan via wargaming;

		- (GT0470) defeating adversary attempts to hide malicious content and behaviour by:

		- identifying anti-forensics and anti-analysis techniques at scale;

		- conducting anomaly detection and review;

		- building hypotheses of likely adversary activity;

		- executing likely adversary activity to determine indicators;

		- searching for these indicators across a network; and

		- building a better hypothesis by using information gained.

		- Remarks: Task a.1 is intended to be practical. Sr Cyber Def Analysts will be given a step-by-step guide for each attack and implement it. Task a.2.a should be a known type of vulnerability (e.g., suid abuse, toc/tou, buffer overflow etc.) but should not have an existing exploit. Tasks c and d should be taught iteratively – Sr Cyber Def Analysts should implement an attack or masquerading method and then immediately work on detecting it. This PO should include applied exercises in which Sr Cyber Def Analysts take turns running stealthy attacks and attempting to detect them.

## PO 007 Analyze Non-IT Systems

		- Performance: Analyze non-IT systems.

		- Conditions:

		- given:

		- simulated Platform Technologies (PT) / Operational Technologies (OT) systems;

		- open internet connection;

		- IM/IT and ancillary equipment;

		- software and tools;

		- supervision;

		- work area;

		- network diagrams; and

		- references;

		- denied: nil; and

		- environment: day or night, under any weather conditions, within a technical environment.

		- Standard: The Sr Cyber Def Analyst shall analyze non-IT systems IAW refs (D43 and D51-D59) by:

		- analyzing PT and OT systems by:

		- (GT0070) characterizing network traffic to include:

		- OT protocols, to include:

		- Distributed Network Protocol 3 (DNP3); and

		- Modbus;

		- PT protocols, to include:

		- 1553;

		- CANBus; and 

		- Integrated Platform Management Systems (IMPS);

		- characterizing host logs to include:

		- embedded devices, to include:

		- minimal Linux based systems;

		- Busybox; and

		- other Real Time Open Source (RTOS) architectures;

		- Linux forensic logging, to include:

		- common log file locations;

		- Kernel ring buffer logs; and

		- other log information, to include:

		- message logs;

		- sensor logs;

		- device activity logs; and

		- data historians;

		- (GT0080) analyzing network traffic by:

		- applying security monitoring to OT/PT systems;

		- detecting anomalies in network traffic using metadata; and

		- (GT0110) analyzing anomalies in network traffic using metadata;

		- analyzing host logs by:

		- applying security monitoring to OT/PT systems;

		- analyzing anomalies in host logs using metadata; and

		- detecting anomalies in host logs;

		- applying software security principles, to include:

		- software attack vectors;

		- embedded system vulnerabilities;

		- binary exploitation; and

		- fuzzing;

		- explaining hardware security, to include:

		- Printed Circuit Board (PCB) security and countermeasures;

		- firmware extraction; and

		- communication interception;

		- conducting threat emulation on OT and PT systems by:

		- (FT0350) determining configuration changes required to conduct network evaluation by:

		- applying digital forensics for PT/OT systems;

		- explaining system recovery for PT/OT systems; and

		- (GT0010) analyzing persistent collection deficiencies for PT/OT systems;

		- (FT0360) determining configuration changes required to conduct incident response by:

		- applying digital forensics for PT/OT systems;

		- explaining system recovery for PT/OT systems; and

		- (GT0010) analyzing persistent collection deficiencies for PT/OT systems;

		- applying OT specific attacks by:

		- gaining access to an OT network;

		- establishing C2 to a compromised OT node;

		- applying defense evasion techniques to avoid detection;

		- executing a desired effect on the target OT network, to include:

		- device restarts;

		- data disruption;

		- device masquerading;

		- remote control; and

		- system destabilization.

		- Remarks: This module is expected to confirm the Sr Cyber Def Analyst’s ability to apply their knowledge and skills in new situations as well as introduce PT/OT systems. The Sr Cyber Def Analyst is not expected to operate independently on PT/OT and will require the assistance of a system engineer and a cyber officer to establish any new monitoring capabilities. The purpose of para b. is to demonstrate how simple these effects are to achieve in most PT/OT environments. The Cyber Operator will not demonstrate these effects on live systems due to significant risk of causing additional unintended malfunction. Task para-a.1.and para-a.1. b. the lists are not exhaustive and will be required to be updated as new things are coming and out of service. 

		

## PO 008 Defend a Network under Attack

		- Performance: Defend a network under attack.

		- Conditions:

		- given:

		- threat information;

		- mission scenarios;

		- internet connection;

		- IM/IT and ancillary equipment;

		- software and tools;

		- supervision;

		- work area;

		- network diagrams; and

		- references;

		- denied: nil; and

		- environment: day or night, under any weather conditions, within an operational environment.

		- Standard: The Sr Cyber Def Analyst shall defend a network under attack IAW refs (D43, D51 and D52) by:

		- (BT0020) planning tactical cyber activities to include:

		- identifying likely attack vectors to include:

		- identifying network configuration by:

		- (GT0190) analyzing internal network for compromise potential; and

		- (GT0340) analyzing internal cyber event and incident reports in support of mission objectives;

		- (BT0230) advising on cyber threats by:

		- (GT0180) evaluating all source cyber threat information;

		- identifying targets;

		- identifying vectors; and

		- identifying intent;

		- (BT0300) advising on cyber defence enhancements by:

		- (GT0010) analyzing persistent collection deficiencies;

		- (FT0350) determining configuration changes required to conduct network evaluation, to include:

		- host hardening;

		- server hardening;

		- service hardening;

		- enhancing logging;

		- host agent deployment;

		- types of host agents such as AV, EDR, DFIR and decisions between them;

		- network sensor deployment; and 

		- tap, span, r-span, netflow, and decisions between them; and

		- (FT0360) determining configuration changes required to conduct incident response;

		- developing a mission specific toolkit by:

		- (FT0370) developing a deployable cyber defence toolkit;

		- (FT0380) maintaining a deployable cyber defence toolkit;

		- (FT0390) developing a deployable digital forensics toolkit; and

		- (FT0400) maintaining a deployable digital forensics toolkit;

		- monitoring network and host alerts by:

		- (BT0390) validating network alerts through coordination with enterprise-wide cyber defence staff;

		- evaluating true/false positives;

		- tuning network and host alert rules; and

		- (FT0210) recommending approval of deployment of network sensor rules;

		- (BT0350) responding to a cyber event by:

		- conducting initial network scale triage;

		- maintaining Situational Awareness (SA) throughout a compromise;

		- conducting threat hunts and iterative investigations;

		- (GT0040) identifying means of compromise;

		- (GT0150) determining adversary TTPs;

		- extracting considerations for live adversaries and ongoing attacks; and

		- (BT0220) informing direct supervisor on cyber threat(s).

		- Remarks: Pre PLQ Sr-Cyber Def Analysts will be members of a team but are exposed to the planning process as there is no further general training in DP2. Post PLQ Sr Cyber Def Analysts can be expected to lead small party tasks in cyber. 

