| 49 | Reviewed tasks and subtasks to reflect incident response and the tasks required of the members to understand and successfully employ their knowledge and skills are tested during the PC. | Nil |
| 50 | Engaged in discussion about the feasibility of the level of network that the students will have to build and defend. The expectation of the student is that they are able to setup a small network, harden and defend it. Create/deploy a GPO in order to have software installed through the GPO. For the exercise the students should be provided a network. Each student will be responsible for hardening a specific service/server and they will collectively defend the network under supervision of a techxorcist or equivalent. Board consensus, all approved changes. | Nil |
| 51 | Boards starts reviewing Annex F – Training delivery reform potential. PO 001 – 008 will be taught by RMC, however if they are unavailable, Annex F has listed a number of fairly equal courses that are commercial. SANS, OSCP, Netrunner, as examples. Board continued to populate the table in Annex F, providing a number of alternatives, commercially available courses that would satisfy the PO and either certify or be required to take an assessment afterwards. | Nil |
|  | DAY 4 |  |
| 52 | Review of Day3 RoD Board agrees on RoD |  |
| 53 | Discussed equivalences and added cert codes to the courses under remarks in the Annex. Also commenting that in order to be successful the POs should be done in order from 1 to 8. Who decides when a packeteer is ready to go on the DP2 course; a team (Mentor team) may end up being in charge of the course loading and training. Board discussed pre-req for attending DP2, possibility of DP1 + work in the environment for a min of 12 months. Board discussed that depending on experience at CFNOC 12 months may not be sufficient as the only relevant experience is hands on technical practice. Board decided that the most appropriate route is a formal pre-course package to ensure they receive all expected experience as a packeteer. Board discussed likely failure rate. There are going to be failures and this needs to be explained to CoC that not everyone has the aptitude to continue as a Cyber Operator. The cyber operator COT and intake processes do not ensure that there is aptitude to achieve competency in the trade. An OJPR will lower the failure rate by ensuring packeteers have the required baseline competence to continue learning. There are voiced issues that if going through COTS courses it will take significantly longer to complete then just the RMC course. The commercial course will also lack the scholastic environment provided by a proper training establishment. | Instructional staff to review after pilot serial |
| 54 | QL4/OJPR was revisited. The board agrees that there is a requirement to have this implemented before mbrs are loaded onto the DP2. The board estimates that there are some mbrs at CFNOC who could immediately complete an OJPR based on their prior experience. The board discussed that if the DP2 is approved and contracted prior to the OJPR that the CAF should accept the increased risk of failure by sending packeteers as they are available. | Instructional staff to review after pilot serial |
| 55 | Continued review of POs. Changed some wording to better reflect intent of POs and verified that remarks accurately reflected and explained any vague comments. | Nil |
|  | DAY 5 |  |
| 56 | Review of Day4 RoD Board agrees on RoD | Nil |
| 57 | Remark added to TDR noting that the suggested OJPR should be centrally managed and tracked. | Nil |
| 58 | Board opened day 5 with discussions on OJPR and what it should look like while fleshing out a rough draft. The board agrees that there is a gap between DP1 and DP2 due to the lack of a large and realistic training environment. The DP1 scenarios lack false positives in most scenarios and dealing with them is a significant part of real world cyber. The board agrees that the DP2 COTS option is likely to have a high failure rate, and this can be mitigated by ensuring packeteers get an appropriate breadth of experience in real world cyber before commencing. This will lower the failure rate, resulting in significant cost savings for the CAF and better morale for the trade. As part of the draft, the board created a table to identify the delta’s between DP1 and DP2. This will help build the OJPR, eliminate the deltas and allow the member to refresh their skills. | Instructional staff to assist TDO in producing a draft OJPR |
| 59 | The board drafted some likely points which should be included in a pre-course package. The board agrees this should be done post OFP but prior to DP2. It should have its own MITE code and be administered by the training authority.  The intent of the pre-course package would be to have the candidate’s supervisor sign off on a form indicating that the candidate has in fact had the opportunity to work within a specified list of job roles for enough time to ensure they have a general understanding of how that job role contributes to the operation of the overall organization. This completed form would be sent to the CTU, where the candidate would be granted a MITE code, thereby clearing them to proceed to DP2. D Cyber FD and the CFSCE cyber det will finalize this action item. | Instructional staff to review after pilot serial |
|  | Post-Board |  |
|  | MPGTG HQ converted QSP to QS format. Details captured in Chap 3 of TP are attached below: TRAINING STRATEGY The training requirement will be achieved through a Rationalized Training Delivery (RTD): Mod 1: This module will be a pre-course package to be completed at the unit prior to candidates being loaded on module 2; Mod 2 – 9 – RTD: These modules will be taught by Royal Military College (RMC): and/or Entail contractor developed and delivered materials aligned with Cyber Op training and education requirements; TRAINING DURATION Training and education for POs 001–008 will be delivered by RMC and or Entail contractor developed and delivered materials aligned with Cyber Op training and education requirements. As such, no EOs have been developed for POs 001–008 given the difficulty involved in developing a training plan flexible enough to account for all program designs. Additionally, there is currently a lack of subject matter experts within the CAF to develop the content. Based on availability at RMC to accommodate. Permission is required from the TA and D Cyber Ops FD to deviate from the prescribed course capacity. COURSE LOADING The authority to load members onto this course lies with the Cyber Ops Career Manager. SCHEDULING Training will be scheduled by the Combat Training Centre (CTC) in MITE, in consultation with MPGTG HQ and D Cyber Ops FD, to meet the training requirements. PREREQUISITES To be selected for this course the loading authority must ensure that candidates have completed the Basic Military Qualification (BMQ) course (AINT) and Cyber Defence Analyst (ALJQ). QUALIFICATION Successful completion of this course constitutes the primary means of attaining the CYBER OP RQ CPL (AMCN). Additionally, the following MITE eight course IDs will be granted: XXXXXX. PRIOR LEARNING ASSESSMENT AND RECOGNITION (PLAR) PLARs shall be conducted in accordance with DAOD 5031-1 – Canadian Forces Military Equivalencies Program and CFITES Volume 12 – Canadian Forces Military Equivalencies Programme: Prior Learning Assessment. |  |

